= Sage (Salvia officinalis)

_Scope: This entry treats Salvia officinalis L. — common / garden sage, the Latin-Italian-Portuguese salvia proper — and its drug, the dried leaf (Salviae folium). It is a warm-dry Mediterranean Lamiaceae in the same register as oregano and mint, and shares the thujone-bearing essential oil (and its caution) with wormwood. Three disambiguations are load-bearing: (1) the Chinese pharmacy "salvia" is a completely different drug — 丹参 (dānshēn), the root of Salvia miltiorrhiza, a blood-invigorating TCM medicinal, not this aromatic leaf; (2) rosemary has recently been reclassified into Salvia (S. rosmarinus) but is not what "salvia" denotes; (3) the psychoactive S. divinorum is a distinct Mazatec species. Status: deeply classical in Greco-Roman medicine (Dioscorides' ἐλελίσφακον, and the Salernitan sage maxim) and well-developed in Tibb (maryamiyya / qaṣʿīn); not classical in Chinese medicine (a Mediterranean plant, outside the Bencao tradition — and not to be confused with danshen) or in Ayurveda (a post-classical European introduction) — those sections are reasoned extension, flagged as such._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Salvia officinalis_ L., family Lamiaceae (Labiatae); the drug is the *dried leaf* (_Salviae folium_). The genus name _salvia_ is from Latin _salvus_ ("healthy, whole, safe") / _salvēre_ ("to be well"), via the plant's reputation as a healer — so the name itself means, roughly, "the saving/healing herb," and is cognate with English _salvation_, _salve_, and _safe_. _officinalis_ = "of the _officīna_" (the monastic storeroom/dispensary), the standard epithet marking an official medicinal.#footnote[Etymology of _salvia_ < _salvus_/_salvēre_ ("to be well/whole," hence "the healing herb"): Lewis & Short, _A Latin Dictionary_ (s.v. _salvia_, _salvus_); de Vaan, _Etymological Dictionary of Latin_ (s.v. _salvus_). _officinalis_ < _officīna_ (dispensary/storeroom); cf. the same epithet in _licorice-root.typ_'s _Glycyrrhiza glabra_ discussion. The name encodes the panacea reputation crystallized in the Salernitan maxim (below).]

*English:* _Sage_ — from Old French _sauge_, from Latin _salvia_; the modern English form is a worn-down borrowing of the same Latin word. (The homograph _sage_ "wise person" is unrelated — from Latin _sapere_, "to be wise" — though folk association with the herb's memory-strengthening reputation is old and persistent; flagged as coincidence, not etymology.) Italian, Spanish, and Portuguese keep the Latin word almost intact: _salvia_ / _salvia_ / _sálvia_.#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "sage, n.²" the plant, < Old French _sauge_ < Latin _salvia_; distinct from "sage, n.¹/adj." the wise person, < Latin _sapere_). The two English words are unrelated in origin; the memory-herb association is a folk overlay — flagged.]

*Greek:* *ἐλελίσφακον (elelísphakon)* and *σφάκος (sphákos)* — Dioscorides' and Theophrastus' names for sage; the word is of *uncertain, probably pre-Greek origin*. Modern Greek *φασκόμηλο (faskómilo)* continues _sphakos_.#footnote[Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ III.33 (_elelísphakon_); the word's pre-Greek/obscure origin: Beekes, _Etymological Dictionary of Greek_ (s.v. σφάκος, ἐλελίσφακον). Modern Greek _faskómilo_ preserves the ancient root.]

*Chinese:* _S. officinalis_ is *not classical* and must be sharply distinguished from the classical Chinese _Salvia_. The modern Chinese name for garden sage is *鼠尾草 (shǔwěicǎo, "rat-tail herb")* or *藥用鼠尾草 (yàoyòng shǔwěicǎo)* — a modern botanical rendering, *not* a _Bencao_ drug. The genuinely classical Chinese _Salvia_ is a *different species and a different part*: *丹参 (dānshēn, "cinnabar root/ginseng")*, the *root* of _Salvia miltiorrhiza_ Bunge, a major blood-invigorating (活血) medicinal — see the disambiguation note in the CM section below.#footnote[鼠尾草 (_S. officinalis_) as a modern non-classical name, vs. the classical 丹参 (_S. miltiorrhiza_ root) blood-invigorating drug: Bensky, Clavey & Stöger, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Materia Medica_, 3rd ed. (Eastland Press, 2004). The two share a genus but nothing else clinically — the leaf-aromatic sage of the West is not danshen.]

*Sanskrit:* *No classical name* — _S. officinalis_ is a European Lamiaceae, post-classical in India (introduced via European trade and later Unani/colonial channels). Modern Hindi/Indian usage borrows *सेज (sej)* or *साल्विया (sālviyā)* / renders it as _kamarkas_-adjacent culinary sage; treated as reasoned-only below.

*Arabic / Persian / Urdu (Tibb):* *مَريَميّة (maryamiyya, "Mary's herb")* — the living Levantine name (the herb of _shāy maryamiyya_, sage tea); *قَصْعين (qaṣʿīn)* and *سالميّة (sālmiyya)* in the classical/pharmacy register; also *ناعمة (nāʿima)*. Persian: *مریم‌گلی (maryam-golī)*. Urdu/Hakeemi: *سیج (sej)*, *قصعین (qaṣʿīn)*. See `tibb-al-arabi.typ`.#footnote[_Maryamiyya_ ("Mary's herb," the Levantine sage of _shāy maryamiyya_), _qaṣʿīn_, and _sālmiyya_ for _S. officinalis_ (and closely related eastern-Mediterranean sages such as _S. fruticosa_/_S. triloba_, the Levantine "Greek sage" often sold as maryamiyya): Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_ (_qaṣʿīn_); modern Levantine ethnobotany. The _maryamiyya_ name is a Christian-Levantine Marian folk association, not a Qurʾānic/Prophetic attestation — flagged in the Tibb section. Note the species blur: much commercial Levantine "maryamiyya" is _S. fruticosa_, a sibling species.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_Salvia officinalis has no classical Chinese entry_ — it is a Mediterranean Lamiaceae outside the _Bencao_ tradition. *A hard disambiguation first:* the classical Chinese drug called "salvia" in English herb references is *丹参 (dānshēn)*, the *root* of _Salvia miltiorrhiza_ — bitter, slightly cold, entering Heart and Liver, a premier *活血化瘀 (huó xuè huà yū,* invigorate-blood, dispel-stasis) drug for chest _bì_/angina, menstrual stasis, and _restless-Heart_ patterns. *That is a different species, a different part (root not leaf), and a different action from garden sage, and the two must not be merged.* The reading below is *reasoned extension* for _S. officinalis_ the leaf-aromatic, on classical axes, by analogy to other warm-pungent-aromatic Lamiaceae (cf. `oregano.typ`, `mint.typ`) — flagged, not canonical.

*Property (by reasoned extension):* Warm (溫), tending neutral; aromatic-drying.

*Flavor:* Pungent (辛) and bitter (苦), aromatic.

*Channels (by extension):* Lung (肺), Spleen (脾), Liver (肝).

*Actions (by extension):*
- *解表 (jiě biǎo)* — releases the exterior (cold-pattern chills, early sore throat).
- *止汗 (zhǐ hàn)* — *stops sweating* — a striking reasoned convergence: the Western antihidrotic use of sage maps directly onto the 止汗 category (the domain of drugs like 麻黃根 mahuang-gen and 浮小麥, floating wheat), and modern pharmacology confirms it (below). See `wheat.typ` for 浮小麥.
- *化痰利咽 (huà tán lì yān)* — transforms phlegm, benefits the throat (the gargle use).
- *醒腦 / 益智 (xǐng nǎo / yì zhì)* — awakens the brain / benefits the wits (the memory reputation).

*Cautions (by extension):* yin-vacuity heat and hot patterns (the warm-drying-aromatic profile); pregnancy and lactation (emmenagogue and milk-drying — see below); the thujone ceiling on dose (cf. `wormwood.typ`).

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_Reasoned analysis, not classical._ Salvia officinalis _is a post-classical European introduction with no entry in Caraka, Suśruta, or the nighaṇṭus. The reading below applies dravyaguna principles by extension, in the register of the other reasoned-only Lamiaceae entries (`oregano.typ`, `mint.typ`)._

*Reasoned rasa:* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent), Tikta (तिक्त, bitter), Kaṣāya (कषाय, astringent — the antihidrotic/binding note).

*Reasoned guṇa:* Laghu (लघु, light), Rūkṣa (रूक्ष, dry), Tīkṣṇa (तीक्ष्ण, sharp), Sugandhi (सुगन्धि, fragrant).

*Reasoned vīrya:* Uṣṇa (उष्ण, heating) — converging with the Tibb Hot and Galenic warm-dry readings.

*Reasoned vipāka:* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent).

*Prabhāva:* None classically (no classical entry). If singled out, the *antihidrotic (sveda-stambhana) potency* — a genuine, pharmacologically real drying of sweat not obviously predictable from a warm-pungent aromatic — is the candidate prabhāva-level feature; flagged as modern interpretive.

*Reasoned doṣa effect:* Kapha-Vāta-śāmaka (the warm-pungent-dry profile clears Kapha and, via the uṣṇa note, calms Vāta); Pitta-vardhaka in excess (uṣṇa-tīkṣṇa).

*Reasoned karma:*
- _Kaṇṭhya, svarya_ — throat- and voice-benefiting (the gargle register).
- _Svedaghna / sveda-stambhana_ — antihidrotic (the signature action; cf. `wheat.typ`'s _svedahara_ floating-wheat parallel).
- _Medhya, smṛti-prada_ — intellect- and memory-promoting (the sage-and-memory convergence).
- _Dīpana, pācana_ — digestive-carminative.
- _Kaphahara, kāsahara_ — anti-phlegm, anti-cough.
- _Stanya-śoṣaṇa / stanya-nāśana_ — *milk-drying* (galactofuge — the traditional Western use to stop lactation).#footnote[Reasoned placement anchors on the pharmacologically real antihidrotic and galactofuge effects and the memory register; it is *not* a classical Ayurvedic entry — flagged. The _svedaghna_/_sveda-stambhana_ reading parallels the 中醫 止汗 category and the floating-wheat (浮小麥) antihidrotic drug in `wheat.typ`.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(no classical viruddha for S. officinalis — extensions of conditional rules; tags S core saṃhitā, N nighaṇṭu, F folk, M modern/pharmacological)_

- *(extended) Conditional:* Pitta excess and hot-pattern conditions (the uṣṇa-tīkṣṇa profile); high Vāta only via the rūkṣa edge.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological:*
  - *Pregnancy and lactation* — *contraindicated at medicinal/essential-oil dose*: emmenagogue tradition, thujone neurotoxicity, and the milk-suppressing effect (culinary amounts are safe; therapeutic doses and the oil are not).
  - *Epilepsy / seizure disorders* — caution: like wormwood, sage oil contains *thujone*, a GABA-A antagonist and convulsant (see `wormwood.typ`); high-dose or prolonged use is the risk, not culinary use.
  - *Prolonged / high-dose essential-oil use* — avoid: cumulative thujone toxicity.
  - *Lamiaceae allergy* — possible cross-reactivity (mint family).

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

Sage is *classical in Tibb* under *قَصْعين (qaṣʿīn)* and the living Levantine *مَريَميّة (maryamiyya)* — a warm-drying *brain-and-nerve tonic, astringent, and throat-and-digestive aromatic*, inherited from the Greek _elelísphakon_ and elaborated in the Arabic and Levantine tradition. See `tibb-al-arabi.typ`.

*Arabic:* *مَريَميّة (maryamiyya)*, *قَصْعين (qaṣʿīn)*, *سالميّة (sālmiyya)*. Persian: *مریم‌گلی (maryam-golī)*. Urdu/Hakeemi: *سیج (sej)*, *قصعین (qaṣʿīn)*.

*Mizāj:* *Hot and Dry in the 2nd degree* (the standard placement for this warm-drying aromatic; sources vary between 1st and 2nd degree for the heat) — converging with Galen and diverging from nothing major here.#footnote[Mizāj and actions of _qaṣʿīn_/_maryamiyya_: Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_ (_qaṣʿīn_); later Unani (_Khazāyin al-Adwiya_); the Hot-Dry placement (1st–2nd degree) is standard, with some variation across editions — flagged for verification against a specific edition.]

*Afʿāl (actions):*
- *Muqawwī al-dimāgh wa-l-aʿṣāb* — tonic of the brain and nerves (the signature Tibb action; the memory-and-cognition register).
- *Qābiḍ* — astringent; *ḥābis li-l-ʿaraq* — *sweat-arresting* (the antihidrotic action, the Tibb correlate of the Western and reasoned-Chinese use).
- *Muqawwī al-maʿida*, *hāḍim*, *kāsir li-l-riyāḥ* — stomach tonic, digestive, carminative (the _maryamiyya_ digestive-tea register).
- *Munaqqī li-l-ṣadr* — chest-cleansing; *nāfiʿ li-l-ḥalq* — throat-benefiting (gargle).
- *Mudirr li-l-ḥayḍ* — emmenagogue (hence the pregnancy caution); *muqawwī al-bāh* (a minor attribution).
- *Mujaffif* — desiccant/drying (wounds, oral mucosa).

*Istemālāt (indications):* nervous debility and weak memory; excessive sweat (night sweats, the menopausal register); weak/cold digestion and flatulence; sore throat, mouth ulcers, and gum inflammation (gargle); cough and chest congestion; menstrual retention.

*Mawāneʿ / Iḥtirāzāt (contraindications):* hot/bilious (_ṣafrāwī_) temperaments in quantity; pregnancy (emmenagogue) and nursing (milk-drying); excess causes headache in hot constitutions; the thujone caution on prolonged high dose.

*Muṣliḥ (correctives):* a little honey, gum tragacanth (_kathīrāʾ_), or a cooling aromatic (rose-water) to temper the drying heat. *Badal (substitutes):* other warm Lamiaceae aromatics — *marzanjūsh* (marjoram/oregano-class, see `oregano.typ`), *zaʿtar*.

*Ṭibb al-Nabawī attestation:* *none proper.* Sage is a *Greco-Arabic (Dioscoridean) and Levantine-folk* drug, not a Qurʾānic or sound-hadith substance — I flag this rather than manufacture a pedigree. The name *maryamiyya* ("Mary's herb") is a *Christian-Levantine Marian folk association* (a legend that the Virgin Mary blessed the plant), *not* an Islamic-Prophetic attestation — an important honesty point given how often the name is read as devotional provenance.#footnote[Sage has no Qurʾānic or sound-hadith attestation; it is a Greco-Arabic and Levantine-folk drug. The _maryamiyya_ ("Mary's herb") name reflects a Levantine Christian Marian legend, not Ṭibb al-Nabawī — flagged so the devotional-sounding name is not mistaken for Prophetic provenance. Tibb sources: Ibn al-Bayṭār, _al-Jāmiʿ_; _Khazāyin al-Adwiya_.]

== Classical Formulas & Preparations

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

No classical Chinese formula uses _S. officinalis_. The classical Chinese _Salvia_ formulas belong to *丹参* (_S. miltiorrhiza_ root) and are noted only to keep the disambiguation sharp: *丹参飲 (Dānshēn Yǐn)* for blood-stasis chest/epigastric pain, and danshen's modern cardiovascular use (e.g. the compound _Fufang Danshen_ preparations). These have *nothing to do with garden sage*; modern Chinese use of 鼠尾草 (garden sage) is post-classical and Western-derived.#footnote[丹参飲 and danshen's blood-invigorating formulas: Bensky & Barolet, _Chinese Herbal Medicine: Formulas & Strategies_, 2nd ed. (Eastland Press, 2009). Listed only to enforce the danshen-vs-garden-sage disambiguation; _S. officinalis_ has no classical Chinese formulary.]

=== Ayurveda

No classical Ayurvedic formula uses _S. officinalis_ (post-classical introduction). Modern Indian/Western herbal use slots it into the _medhya_ (nootropic) and _kaṇṭhya_ (throat) registers by extension — reasoned, not classical.

=== Western / Greco-Roman and later European medicine

*The deepest and most continuous tradition for this herb.*

- *Greco-Roman:* *Dioscorides* (_De Materia Medica_ III.33, _elelísphakon_) treats sage for the retention of the menses, for wounds, for coughs, and as an astringent; *Pliny* (_Naturalis Historia_ XXII) gives diuretic, emmenagogue, styptic, and memory-and-nerve uses; *Galen* classes it warm-and-drying. The plant carried a *panacea* reputation from antiquity, encoded in its very name (_salvia_ < _salvēre_).
- *The Salernitan maxim:* the _Regimen Sanitatis Salernitanum_ (Schola Medica Salernitana, 12th–13th c.) gives the famous line *"Cur moriatur homo cui salvia crescit in horto?"* — "Why should a man die when sage grows in his garden?" — the epitome of sage's medieval standing as a near-universal remedy.
- *Medieval / early-modern pharmacy:* sage was officinal (_Salviae folium_); a *sage-and-vinegar gargle* for sore throat and mouth ulcers, sage tea for night sweats and weak digestion, and sage as an antihidrotic and a galactofuge (to *dry up milk* at weaning) are all long-standing. Sage was one of the aromatic herbs of the plague-vinegars (*Four Thieves' Vinegar*, alongside wormwood, rosemary, and others; cf. `wormwood.typ`), and a strewing/preservative herb. Culpeper and Gerard treat it extensively; the English "sage strengthens the memory" topos is continuous from Greek antiquity.
- *Modern phytotherapy survivals:* sage has an *EMA/HMPC traditional-use* standing for *relief of excessive sweating* (its best-supported modern use), for *minor oral/throat inflammation* (sage gargles and lozenges), and for mild dyspepsia; sage extracts are studied for *cognition/memory* (cholinesterase inhibition — the modern echo of the ancient memory reputation).#footnote[Greco-Roman: Dioscorides, _De Materia Medica_ III.33 (Beck translation, Olms-Weidmann); Pliny, _Naturalis Historia_ XXII (Loeb); Galen, _De simplicium medicamentorum facultatibus_. The Salernitan maxim: _Regimen Sanitatis Salernitanum_ (standard editions). Officinal preparations, Four-Thieves vinegar, and the galactofuge/antihidrotic uses: Culpeper, _The English Physician_; Gerard, _Herball_; Evans, _Trease and Evans' Pharmacognosy_. Modern standing: EMA/HMPC assessment report and monograph on _Salvia officinalis_ folium (hyperhidrosis; oral/throat inflammation); cognition studies — verify against current literature.]

=== Ṭibb (Arabic-Islamic medicine)

Covered above: *qaṣʿīn / maryamiyya* as a brain-and-nerve tonic, astringent, sweat-arrester, and throat-and-digestive aromatic. The canonical living preparation is *شاي مريمية (shāy maryamiyya)* — Levantine *sage tea*, drunk for digestion, sore throat, colds, and menstrual cramps, and often blended into or served alongside mint tea and green/black tea across the Levant. Sage also enters compound *maʿjūn* and *jawārish* aromatics as a warming-tonic component.

== Climate, Constitution & Regional Diet

_S. officinalis_ is a *warm-dry Mediterranean* Lamiaceae — native to the northern Mediterranean limestone hills (the Balkans, Italy, the Dalmatian coast, Iberia) and cultivated across the Mediterranean and temperate world. Its thujone-and-camphor-rich oil is a hot-dry-summer, sunny-slope adaptation, in the same climatic register as oregano and thyme. See `climates.typ`.

=== Warm-dry Mediterranean (the canonical home)

Balkan/Dalmatian, Italian, Iberian, and Levantine dry-summer hills (Köppen Csa/Csb). Constitutionally the warm-dry summer carries Pitta and external 燥/熱; the Levantine winter carries cool-damp. *Traditional preparation and cares.* Sage is a *warming-drying culinary-and-medicinal herb* used with rich, fatty, or cold-damp foods: the Italian _saltimbocca_ and _burro e salvia_ (sage-butter) pairings with veal and pasta, sage with pork, goose, sausage, and beans across Europe — a warming-carminative _muṣliḥ_ for heavy meats. Medicinally, the Levantine *shāy maryamiyya* and the European sage tea/gargle are the constitutive forms. The drying-warming profile is climatically apt for cool/damp seasons and cold-pattern digestion; heavy use in hot-Pitta constitutions or high summer should be moderated, and the classical tradition tempers it with cooling adjuvants (lemon, the honey/rose-water _muṣliḥ_).

=== Climates where sage is not traditional

Hot-humid tropical, cold-dry high-altitude, and cold-damp Atlantic zones have no indigenous _S. officinalis_ tradition; its spread there is post-classical (culinary-commercial and horticultural). The warming-antihidrotic-digestive _indications_ travel, but the plant's constitutional home is warm-dry Mediterranean slope.

=== Modern transposition (_deśa-viruddha_ / _kāla-viruddha_, Caraka Sūtra 26)

Globalized cooking carries sage into every climate, mostly as a *culinary aromatic* (safe at food dose) — the transposition issue is less geographic than one of *form and dose*: the modern *high-dose sage essential-oil supplement* register (for sweating or cognition) concentrates thujone far outside the traditional food-and-tea context, and the same _mātrā_/thujone caution that governs wormwood applies. Seasonally, the warming-drying herb suits the cool/damp season and cold-pattern digestion more than high summer in hot constitutions (the _kāla-viruddha_ parallel). See `climates.typ`.

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Sage is a *culinary-and-medicinal herb*, used in small amounts; its interest is the essential oil and polyphenols, and its pharmacology carries a thujone safety signal shared with wormwood.

*Key constituents:*
- *Essential oil (1–2.5% of dried leaf)* — dominated (chemotype-dependent) by *α- and β-thujone* (the toxic monoterpene ketones, as in wormwood), *1,8-cineole (eucalyptol)*, *camphor*, *borneol*, *α-/β-pinene*, and *bornyl acetate*. Thujone content varies widely by chemotype and is the limiting safety compound; the related *Salvia lavandulifolia* (Spanish sage) is *thujone-poor* and is preferred for some cognition studies.
- *Phenolic acids* — *rosmarinic acid* (the headline polyphenol, anti-inflammatory/antioxidant), caffeic and chlorogenic acids.
- *Diterpenes* — *carnosic acid* and *carnosol* (antioxidant diterpenes, shared with rosemary).
- *Triterpenes* — ursolic and oleanolic acids; *flavonoids* (luteolin, apigenin glycosides).#footnote[Phytochemistry (α-/β-thujone, 1,8-cineole, camphor; rosmarinic acid; carnosic acid/carnosol) and the thujone-poor _S. lavandulifolia_ used in cognition trials: standard pharmacognosy (Evans, _Trease and Evans' Pharmacognosy_); EMA/HMPC assessment on _Salvia officinalis_; current reviews — verify against current literature. Thujone is the shared toxic principle with wormwood (see `wormwood.typ`).]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Antihidrotic (antihyperhidrotic) — the best-supported modern use, and a clean cross-system convergence.* Sage genuinely *reduces sweating* (relevant to hyperhidrosis and menopausal hot-flush sweats); the mechanism is incompletely settled (antimuscarinic and central components proposed). This is the modern correlate of the Western antihidrotic tradition, the Tibb *ḥābis li-l-ʿaraq*, the reasoned Ayurvedic _svedaghna_, and the reasoned Chinese 止汗 — an unusually tidy four-way convergence.
- *Cognition / cholinergic* — sage inhibits *acetylcholinesterase*; small RCTs (often with thujone-poor _S. lavandulifolia_) report modest improvements in memory/attention and some signal in mild Alzheimer's — the modern echo of the millennia-old memory reputation. Evidence is preliminary; flagged.
- *Antimicrobial / anti-inflammatory (oral/throat)* — sage extracts inhibit oral bacteria and soothe mucosal inflammation, grounding the gargle/lozenge use (EMA traditional use).
- *Antioxidant* — rosmarinic and carnosic acids; robust in vitro, modest clinically.
- *Galactofuge* — the traditional milk-drying effect at weaning; empirically long-attested, mechanism uncertain.
- *Glycemic / lipid* — some preclinical and small-trial signals; modest.

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:* the limiting compound is *thujone*, not leaf weight (cf. `wormwood.typ`).
- *Culinary use* (leaf, gram amounts): unrestricted at normal intake.
- *Sage tea / infusion:* ~*1–3 g dried leaf* per cup, up to ~3×/day, for *short courses*; not continuous high-volume use.
- *Essential oil / high-dose extract:* thujone-limited — the oil should *not* be taken neat or chronically; EU thujone limits for foods/beverages apply to sage-flavored products (as for wormwood).
- *Contraindicated:* *pregnancy and lactation* (emmenagogue, thujone, milk-drying); *epilepsy/seizure disorders* (thujone convulsant).
- *Cautions:* prolonged high-dose or essential-oil use (cumulative thujone — reports of tachycardia, vertigo, and, at extreme dose, seizures); Lamiaceae allergy; the antihidrotic/anticholinergic action warrants care with anticholinergic drugs.
- *Reference thresholds, not personalized medical advice* — culinary sage is safe; concentrated/prolonged medicinal use, and especially the essential oil, warrant care and (in the risk groups) clinician supervision.#footnote[Dosing and safety: EMA/HMPC assessment report and monograph on _Salvia officinalis_ folium and aetheroleum (hyperhidrosis; oral inflammation; the pregnancy/lactation and thujone cautions); EU thujone limits (shared framework with wormwood — see `wormwood.typ`). These are reference thresholds requiring current verification, not medical advice; pregnancy, lactation, and epilepsy are firm contraindications for medicinal doses.]

